            <chunk name="Object" index="2">
              <items count="3">
                <item name="GUID" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">b7e3c1d4-62a9-4f0e-9c35-8d1a2e47f6b3</item>
                <item name="Lib" type_name="gh_guid" type_code="9">82b21a65-da8f-433c-93fb-f9814609e1d6</item>
                <item name="Name" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">City Surface</item>
              </items>
              <chunks count="1">
                <chunk name="Container">
                  <items count="6">
                    <item name="CitySurfaceInputLayoutVersion" type_name="gh_int32" type_code="3">10</item>
                    <item name="ClayLook" type_name="gh_bool" type_code="1">true</item>
                    <item name="Description" type_name="gh_string" type_code="10">Build ONE watertight surface of terrain, buildings (LoD 1.2 boxes to LoD 2.2 roofs) and surface layers (water, roads, green) for urban CFD. Ground comes from points (wired, or class 2 of a LAS/XYZ cloud) and every footprint and layer outline is carried into its triangulation, so buildings stand IN the terrain with walls down to the ground on every side instead of boxes sunk through a plate. A roof is the footprint's Height above its median ground, or else the Roof Percentile of the roof points (class 6) inside it, or — with neither, typically a house the survey saw only as the tree canopy over it — a flat box at the median height of its five nearest reconstructed buildings (the Report counts them). Overlapping footprints are separated (the earlier one wins); flattened layers sit at one level. Outputs are split by part so each can be its own patch. Method: Pađen et al. (2022), Frontiers in Built Environment 8:899332, doi:10.3389/fbuil.2022.899332; Biljecki et al. (2016), Computers, Environment and Urban Systems 59, 25-37, doi:10.1016/j.compenvurbsys.2016.04.005.

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.20.0.827

Version 1.21.0.827</item>
